{
  "url": "https://www.hotspawn.com/counter-strike/guide/are-cs2-skin-changers-bannable",
  "canonical": "https://www.hotspawn.com/counter-strike/guide/are-cs2-skin-changers-bannable",
  "language": "en",
  "title": "Are CS2 Skin Changers Bannable? | Safe Or Not Explained - Hotspawn",
  "description": "CS2 skin changers allow cheaters to make it look like they own any skin in the game. These skins are only visible on the cheater's client, so other players",
  "headings": [
    {
      "level": 1,
      "text": "Are CS2 skin changers bannable? | Safe or not explained"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "text": "CS2 skin changers can earn you a VAC ban"
    }
  ],
  "document_h1": [
    "Are CS2 skin changers bannable? | Safe or not explained"
  ],
  "text": "Are CS2 skin changers bannable? | Safe or not explained\n\nCS2 skin changers allow cheaters to make it look like they own any skin in the game. These skins are only visible on the cheater’s client, so other players will just see default skins on the server. But despite this, it removes a lot of value from Valve’s in-game economy , which you can imagine the developer isn’t too happy about. This leads many players to ask the question, “Are CS2 skin changers bannable?” CS2 skin changers can earn you a VAC ban Yes, CS2 skin changers are bannable. It’s as simple as that. Even using the most popular CS2 skin changers puts you at risk of a VAC ban, which will lead to you being permanently unable to play Counter-Strike on your Steam account. Because Valve is so heavily invested in the CS2 skin economy, it has a vested interest in making sure skin changers are heavily cracked down upon. If you’re asking are CS2 skin changers bannable when considering using them, ask yourself if it’s worth risking your entire account. Of course, we all want to have the best CS2 skins on the server. It’s cool to have your most expensive skins recognized by other players in-game, but because these skins are client-side, you don’t even get that moment using a CS2 skin changer. It’s very high-risk, for very low reward. There are some rare exceptions. Using a third-party client to change your CS2 skins in public matches is bannable, sure, but changing skins in community servers is totally fine. For example, if you want to inspect CS2 skins on an Epidemic server, you’ll run into no issues there. Likewise, xplay servers allow you to play Deathmatch in CS2 with custom skins that you don’t own, and you can’t be banned for that.",
  "words": 321,
